EXT. RAILWAY CUTTING - NIGHT

Frost on the ballast. A burned-out locomotive lies on its side across the tracks, still ticking as the metal cools. Moonlight on the rails.

A squad of six crouches in the lee of the wreck. CAPTAIN HALVARD STRAND, 40s, kneels with a map held under a red-filtered torch. CORPORAL IBRAHIM DAOUD, 30s, scans the embankment through a rifle scope. PRIVATE LENKA VESELY, 20s, holds a radio handset to her ear and mouths silent words. FRIEDRICH "FRITZ" ACKERMANN, 20s, a stretcher-bearer, wraps a bandage around the thigh of a gaunt figure who has not spoken since they found him in the mud: a PRISONER in a torn gray coat. SERGEANT MAEVE O'DONNELL, 50s, the oldest of them, rolls a cigarette she will not light and watches the prisoner's hands.

                    HALVARD
Three kilometers to the bridge.

                    IBRAHIM
They'll have it covered.

                    HALVARD
They'll have everything covered.

Lenka lowers the handset. Shakes her head. No signal.

                    MAEVE
(to herself)
Of course there isn't.

The prisoner lifts his head. Looks at Maeve for the first time. His eyes are pale and amused.

                    PRISONER
You will not make the bridge.

                    FRITZ
He talks.

                    HALVARD
Quiet.

                    PRISONER
I can lead you. A shorter way.

                    HALVARD
Why would you?

                    PRISONER
My name is Anselm. I was a railwayman. This line was mine.

Halvard folds the map. Studies the man. Looks to Maeve, who gives the smallest shrug.

                    ANSELM
There is a service tunnel. Under the cutting. It comes out two hundred meters from the bridge.

                    IBRAHIM
It's a trap.

                    ANSELM
It is a tunnel.

                    LENKA
Captain, I'd rather trust him than the road.

                    HALVARD
You'd rather trust a man in a German coat.

                    ANSELM
It is not a German coat.

                    HALVARD
Then whose is it?

                    ANSELM
Mine.

Maeve lights the cigarette after all. Draws on it once, hands cupped around the ember. Exhales slowly.

                    MAEVE
Let him lead.

                    HALVARD
Sergeant.

                    MAEVE
If he walks us into a gun, I shoot him first.

                    ANSELM
That is fair.

Halvard folds the torch away. Rises. The squad rises with him, silent, shouldering packs and weapons. Fritz hauls Anselm to his feet, and the prisoner, limping, leads the line down the embankment toward a small round hole in the rock.

At its mouth, he turns back to the group and, for the first time, smiles.
